BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SILO NIGHTS at Public Pier: "Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Trav
 el Diary of a Particle"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T230000
UID:travel-diary@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:SILO NIGHTS at Public Pier is curated by OCAD University as pa
 rt of the Public Pier\, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, O
 CAD University\, and Waterfront BIA with the Toronto Port Authority as lea
 d sponsor.\n\nSILO NIGHTS AT PUBLIC PIER\, CURATED BY OCAD UNIVERSITY For 
 six nights only\, Toronto’s waterfront lights up after dark. Starting Se
 ptember 18\, SILO NIGHTS transforms the iconic Canada Malting Silos into a
  giant canvas for immersive art and storytelling — featuring internation
 al artists\, student collaborations\, and Canadian and world premieres. SI
 LO NIGHTS at Public Pier is curated by OCAD University as part of Public P
 ier \, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD University\, 
 and Waterfront BIA with the Toronto Port Authority as lead sponsor. On Fri
 day\, October 2\, from 8 to 11 p.m.\, SILO NIGHTS showcases the Toronto pr
 emiere of Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle . To
 ronto premiere Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle
  Presented with support from the Ontario Arts Council | Conseil des arts d
 e l'Ontario\, Ministère des affaires francophones de l'Ontario\, Déléga
 tion Wallonie Bruxelles\, Wallonie Bruxelles International\, and Wallonie 
 Bruxelles Enseignement. The Toronto premiere of Carnet de voyage d’une p
 articule/Travel Diary of a Particle is a 7-minute animated journey followi
 ng a photon from the sun to earth\, through space and underwater environme
 nts. Exploring light\, nature and ecology\, the project brings together st
 udents from OCAD University’s Experimental Animation program and student
 s from two ARTS² programs in Mons\, Belgium: Digital Arts within Visual A
 rts and Applied and Interactive Music within the Royal Conservatory of Mus
 ic. Students: OCAD University / 2D creation : Shannon Halliday\, Sophia Po
 aps\, Jae Despi\, Laura Barbi\, Aklesiya Yared\, Zahrah Khurram. Students:
  ARTS² / 3D creation: Mathias Serwy\, Natasha Alves de Arujo\, Noah Loute
 \, Lalie Istasse\, Julian Petit\, Eugene Kamegneson Fotso\, Clément Dandr
 imont\, Adriano Spadafora. Students: ARTS² / Sound creation: Oriane Quert
 enmont \, Romain Soudon \, William Escande \, Guillaume Toussaint \, Hugo 
 Drouillon \, Susy Magnien \, Noé Werlen \, Eliza Canivet Faculty: Philipp
 e Blanchard – OCAD University \; Martin Waroux – ARTS²\, Matthieu Buy
 taers – ARTS²\, Cédric Sabato – ARTS²\, Florent Schirrer – ARTS²
 . OCAD U Experimental Animation students with students from the Belgian ar
 t school\, ARTS2. Technology for Silo Nights projections provided by Panas
 onic\, with special thanks to AVA Animation & Visual Arts and the City of 
 Toronto. ABOUT PUBLIC PIER Public Pier is a collaborative public-realm pil
 ot exploring the future potential of Marina Quay West as a vibrant waterfr
 ont destination for culture\, gathering\, and public life. Led by The Bent
 way\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD University\, and the Waterfront BIA\, the
  project uses temporary placemaking and cultural experiences to test how a
 n underused waterfront site can contribute to community life\, economic ac
 tivity\, and the future of Toronto’s public realm. The Toronto Port Auth
 ority is the lead sponsor. Visit Public Pier for the full schedule of prog
 ramming. SILO NIGHTS AT PUBLIC PIER: PROGRAMMING SCHEDULE Friday\, Septemb
 er 18 and Saturday\, September 19 8 to 11 p.m. World premiere Decompositio
 n No. 7: Undergrowth by transmedia artist Ali Phi Curated and presented in
  partnership with The Bentway . This work imagines Toronto as a city shape
 d by what lies beneath it: root systems threading beneath the street grid\
 , connecting trees across the city\, and holding the ground where new grow
 th begins. This new immersive artwork reads the city's street-tree records
 \, including species\, trunk diameter\, and location\, as a score\, transf
 orming data built for maintenance into light\, sound\, and a portrait of s
 low\, ongoing urban growth. Friday\, September 25 8 to 11 p.m. Canadian pr
 emiere Breathe (2026) by Faisal Anwar Presented in partnership with The Bl
 ackwood Gallery at the University of Toronto Mississauga\, in conjunction 
 with Faisal Anwar's CommonSky series featured in the exhibition Lines\, Pa
 ttern\, Action (September–December 2026) at The Blackwood\, curated by R
 uba Kana'an. In the Canadian premiere of Breathe \, by Pakistani-Canadian 
 hybrid artist Faisal Anwar \, the Purple Martin’s 5\,000-mile migration 
 from Brazil to North America transforms the Canada Malting Silos into a li
 ving sky. Through generative visuals\, animation\, sound\, and poetic stor
 ytelling\, the work follows the birds through changing climates and disrup
 ted ecologies. September 26 8 to 11 p.m. World premiere The Shape of Memor
 y by OOOPStudio The world premiere presented by Istituto Italiano di Cultu
 ra – Toronto\, Consulate General of Italy\, Cultural Section\, in collab
 oration with the European Union (EU) through its Delegation to Canada. The
  Shape of Memory treats a city's memory not as history to archive but as i
 ntelligence to activate\, the raw material from which communities build th
 eir next version of themselves. Projected on the Canada Malting Silos\, it
  returns Toronto's memory of land and water to its waterfront. The concept
  for The Shape of Memory was created by OOOPStudio\, with photogrammetry\,
  animation and editing by Alessandro Grisendi and 3D by Marco Noviello. Se
 ptember 26 8 to 11 p.m. World premiere of animation collaboration workshop
  project Presented in partnership with the Embassy of France in Canada and
  Institut français du Canada\, in collaboration with the European Union (
 EU) through its Delegation to Canada. This is the world premiere of an ani
 mation collaboration workshop project\, led by French projection mapping a
 rtist and educator Pablo Gracias with OCAD University students in Experime
 ntal Animation and Graphic Design. October 2 8 to 11 p.m. Toronto premiere
  Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle Presented wit
 h support from the Ontario Arts Council | Conseil des arts de l'Ontario\, 
 Ministère des affaires francophones de l'Ontario\, Délégation Wallonie 
 Bruxelles\, Wallonie Bruxelles International\, and Wallonie Bruxelles Ense
 ignement. Saturday\, October 3\, SILO NIGHTS at Nuit Blanche 7 p.m. to 7 a
 .m. (October 4) Canadian premiere\, The Light World premiere\, Melting Tem
 poralities Two artworks\, projected on the Canada Malting Silos \, will be
  unveiled for Nuit Blanche Toronto\, presented in partnership with the Eur
 opean Union (EU) through its Delegation to Canada and the Global Centre fo
 r Climate Action at OCAD University. The Canadian premiere of The Light \,
  by Paul Murnaghan\, follows a solitary human as she reconnects the consci
 ousness of the Deep Ocean with the subterranean forest floor\, reaffirming
  humanity’s entanglement with worlds that exist beyond our perception. F
 ilmed entirely in natural light\, with underwater sequences captured on a 
 single breath\, the work unfolds as an animist odyssey through water\, lan
 d\, and light. Murnaghan is an Irish multidisciplinary artist\, working wi
 th the substance of belief. He has been exploring\, researching and exhibi
 ting internationally for over twenty years. Current projects focus on anim
 ism and interspecies communication\, attempting a less human-centric view 
 of existence. The world premiere of Melting Temporalities \, by Claire Bru
 net and Johnny Ranger\, transforms the Canada Malting Silos into a monumen
 tal glacier that slowly fractures to reveal forests\, waterways\, geologic
 al formations\, and microscopic life. As the frozen landscape dissolves in
 to digital meshes before reassembling\, the work reflects on climate chang
 e\, resilience\, transformation\, and regeneration.
LOCATION:Off Campus Marina Quay West\, 539 Queens Quay West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/silo-nights-public-pier-ca
 rnet-de-voyage-dune-particule/travel-diary
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
